.TH V32AS 1 "2026-11-09" "v32as 20261109-dev" "Vircon32 assembler manual"
.SH NAME
v32as \- assembler for the Vircon32 console
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v32as
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ FILE ...| \- ]
.br
.B v32as \-C
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.br
.B v32as \-E
.RB [ \-P ]
.RI [ options ]
.IR FILE ...
.br
.B v32as \-T
.RI [ options ]
.IR FILE ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B v32as
assembles Vircon32 assembly language into a VBIN file, the format of a
cartridge program, the same job as the official
.B assemble
tool.
The lexer is generated by flex and the parser by bison.
Its output is byte for byte the output of the official assembler for every
opcode in every legal operand form, for all I/O ports and port values, and for
all data directives.
.PP
Beyond the official language,
.B v32as
has a preprocessor (function\-like macros,
.BR %if ,
.BR %macro ,
.BR %rep ,
and more), extra number forms, local labels, internal register moves, a
relocatable object format with a linker
.RB ( v32asld (1)),
and several debug outputs.
.B \-\-standard
turns every extension off.
The language is summarised in
.BR v32as\-language (7)
and described in full in the README and the files under
.IR docs/ .
.PP
With one input file, the output is named after the input file with its extension
replaced by
.I .vbin
(or
.I .v32ob
with
.BR \-C ).
For example
.B v32as game.asm
writes
.IR game.vbin .
A name without an extension simply gets the extension appended.
Several input files are assembled as one program, in the order given, and share
one preprocessor state (macros, defines, contexts) and one symbol table.
Without a file name, or with
.BR \- ,
the program is read from standard input; the binary is then written to standard
output unless
.B \-o
is given (it is never written to a terminal).
.PP
When standard input is a terminal and no file is given,
.B v32as
starts an interactive mode: each line is assembled as soon as it is entered.
The commands are
.BR .help ,
.BR .labels ,
.BR .dump ,
.BR .reset ,
and
.B .quit
(also
.B .exit
or Ctrl\-D).
.PP
Errors and the debug files refer to the file and line you wrote, also through
.B %include
and macros.
An error inside a macro expansion is reported at the line of the invocation,
followed by a note naming the macro and where it is defined.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be placed before or after the file names.
.SS "Output and target"
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output =\fIFILE\fP
Name the output file.
The default is the input file name with the extension replaced (see above), or
standard output when the input is standard input.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bios
Assemble for the BIOS: addresses start at 0x10000000.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cart
Assemble for a cartridge: addresses start at 0x20000000.
This is the default.
.TP
.BR \-O ", " \-\-origin " \fIADDR\fP"
Assemble for any base address (for example code that will run from RAM).
.I ADDR
is read as a C integer (decimal, 0x hexadecimal or leading\-0 octal) and must
fit in 32 bits.
Cannot be combined with
.B \-b
or
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-\-v32x
Write a v32nix executable (V32X) instead of a VBIN: an 8\-word header
(magic
.IR V32X ,
version 1, header words, program words, data words, entry 0, origin, flags 0)
followed by the program.
Needs
.BR \-\-origin .
The number of zeroed data words comes from
.BR \-\-v32x\-data ,
or else from the source's
.B %define __V32X_DATAWORDS
.IR N ,
which is what
.B v32cc \-\-data\-after\-text
writes (0 if neither is present).
.TP
.BI \-\-v32x\-data= N
The number of data words recorded in the V32X header.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-text
Print a human\-readable listing (the instruction encodings) instead of writing a
VBIN.
Addresses start at 0 unless
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c
or
.B \-O
is given.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-none
Same as
.B \-\-text
with no base address.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-binary
Says what is already the default (a VBIN is written).
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-n
or
.BR \-E .
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-object
Write a relocatable object (FILE.v32ob) for the linker
.BR v32asld (1)
instead of a VBIN.
Labels are relative to the object;
.B global
and
.B extern
name the symbols shared with other objects, and the debug information is stored
in the object.
Not allowed with
.BR \-b ,
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-O ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-n ,
.B \-E
or
.BR \-B ,
nor with
.BR \-\-standard ;
base address, debug files and relocation lists are made by the linker.
.B \-\-map
is allowed.
.SS "Debug and symbol outputs"
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-debug [= \fIMODE\fP]
Also write
.IR OUTPUT .debug
(the output file name plus
.IR .debug ,
for example
.IR game.vbin.debug ),
a CSV file of
.IR address,file,line[,label] .
.I MODE
is
.B program
(the default),
.B vbin
or
.BR v32 ,
as in the official assembler's
.BR "\-g MODE" ;
it may also be given as the next word.
The addresses are absolute word addresses (like 0x20000000) for
.BR program ,
and real byte offsets into the .vbin file (0x0C byte header) or .v32 file
(0x8C byte header) for
.B vbin
and
.BR v32 .
A source file literally named
.IR program ,
.I vbin
or
.I v32
must be written
.IR ./program .
Needs binary output to a file.
See
.B "The debug file"
below.
If the source has
.B ;@var
lines,
.IR OUTPUT .vars
is written too.
If it has a
.B ;@lang X
line, both files start with the comment line
.BR "# lang X" .
Unless
.B \-\-standard
is given,
.IR OUTPUT .sym
is written as well: the functions
.RB ( F ),
compiler variables
.RB ( V )
and data labels
.RB ( R )
as
.IR kind,name,address,words,cfile,cline
with final addresses (the format of v32cc's
.IR NAME .asm.sym ).
.TP
.BI \-\-map= FILE
.I FILE
is the compiler's debug file (CSV
.IR asmfile,asmline,cfile,cline[,function] ,
as
.B v32cc \-g
writes it).
With
.B \-g
the .debug file then names the C file and line (and the function, in the label
column) of each instruction; instructions whose assembly line is not in the map
are left out.
With
.B \-C
the object stores the C lines.
Without
.B \-g
every error and warning is followed by a note such as
.B a.c:123: note: the assembly line above was generated from here
(or
.B it follows the code generated from here
naming the nearest earlier mapped line).
File names are compared exactly first and then by base name (the longest shared
directory tail wins), so
.I main.asm
in the map matches
.IR out/kernel/main.asm .
If no instruction matches at all, a warning lists the names found in the map.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-relocs =\fIFILE\fP
Write the list of words that hold an address (see
.BR "The relocation file" ).
Needs binary output to a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbols= FILE
Write the label table, one
.I address,name,kind
line per label, sorted by address (see
.BR "The symbol file" ).
Not available with
.BR \-C ,
\-E, or the interactive mode (use
.B v32asld \-\-symbols
for objects).
.TP
.B \-\-symbols\-all
With
.BR \-\-symbols :
also list file\-local labels (as
.IR function.local ),
every
.B %define global_NAME
number as kind
.BR V ,
and every
.I DATASYM+N
that the program refers to as
.IR N,DATASYM+N,V .
.TP
.BR \-MD ", " \-MMD
Write the files read (the sources,
.B %include
files and
.B datafile
files) as a Makefile rule, in the output name with
.I .d
in place of the extension unless
.B \-MF
names it.
.B \-MMD
is a synonym (all files are listed).
.TP
.BI \-MF " FILE"
Name the dependency file (implies
.BR \-MD ).
.TP
.BI \-MT " TARGET"
Name the target of the rule (implies
.BR \-MD ).
The default is the output file name.
.TP
.B \-MP
Add an empty rule for each dependency other than the sources named on the
command line.
.SS "Preprocessor"
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-define " \fINAME\fP[=\fIVALUE\fP]"
Predefine a macro (the value defaults to 1).
A
.B %define
of the same name in the source warns and wins, unless the option is written
.BI \-D! NAME = VALUE
(or
.BI \-\-force\-define " NAME=VALUE" ),
when the command line wins.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-undef " \fINAME\fP"
Remove a predefined symbol.
.B \-D
and
.B \-U
are processed in command line order.
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-include " \fIDIR\fP"
Add
.I DIR
to the search path of
.BR "%include <name>" ,
of
.BR "%include \(dqname\(dq" " (after the including file's directory)"
and of
.BR datafile .
May be repeated. After these (and not with
.BR \-\-standard )
v32as also searches the directory the macro libraries were installed in and, next to
the program,
.IR lib ", " ../lib ", " ../share/v32as/lib
and
.IR ../Vircon32/v32tools/include/v32as ,
where they exist.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-preprocess
Only run the preprocessor and print the result (to standard output, or to the
file named by
.BR \-o ).
Lines are preceded by markers of the form
.BR "# LINE \(dqFILE\(dq" .
.TP
.B \-P
With
.BR \-E ,
omit those markers.
.TP
.B \-Wredefine, \-Wno\-redefine
Warn (the default) or do not warn when a
.B %define
changes the value of a macro.
.B \-w
and
.B \-\-standard
also silence it.
.TP
.B \-Wmarkers, \-Wno\-markers
Warn (off by default) about a malformed
.BR ;@src ,
.B ;@var
or
.B ;@scope
line, an unbalanced
.BR ";@scope }" ,
and a block left open.
A malformed
.B ;@src
is treated as
.BR ";@src \-" .
It also warns about a
.B ;@lang
with no name, a second
.B ;@lang
with another name (ignored), and a
.B ;@var
whose
.B loc=
symbol is not known when the file is written.
.SS "ROM definition and build scripts"
These are extensions (an error with
.BR \-\-standard )
and need a BIOS or cartridge VBIN written to a file.
Nothing is written unless one of them is given.
They use what the source says with
.BR %title ,
.BR %version ,
.B %texture
and
.BR %sound ;
see
.BR v32as\-language (7).
.TP
.BI \-\-rom\-xml " FILE"
Also write the ROM definition that
.B packrom
reads.
It names the VBIN, and each image and sound: a
.I .png
or
.I .wav
as the
.I .vtex
or
.I .vsnd
file that
.B png2vircon
or
.B wav2vircon
makes from it, next to the definition; any other file by its path.
Paths are relative to the definition.
.TP
.BI \-\-rom\-xml\-opt " FILE"
The same for the build made from the output of
.BR v32opt :
the VBIN is
.IR NAME Opt.vbin
and the title ends in
.IR " [optimized]" .
.TP
.BR \-\-build\-scripts [= \fIFORMATS\fP]
Write
.I Make.sh
and
.I Make.bat
(FORMATS is
.BR sh ,
.B bat
or
.BR sh,bat ,
the default), and the definitions they use, next to the first source (in its parent for a
.I src/
and
.I obj/
layout).
The scripts run, from the optimizer on:
.B v32as \-E \-P
and
.B v32opt \-O3
(only when it is installed, and given the preprocessed source because it does not know macros or conditionals;
if either fails, only the regular cartridge is built),
.BR v32as ,
.B png2vircon
and
.BR wav2vircon ,
and
.BR packrom ,
which writes
.IR bin/NAME .v32
(and
.IR bin/NAME Opt.v32 ).
Paths in them are relative to the script's directory.
A file called
.I Make.sh
or
.I Make.bat
that does not have the comment line
.I "Generated by v32as"
is never replaced, and a script whose text would not change is not touched.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-build\-scripts
Never write them.
The scripts pass it to the
.B v32as
they run, and set
.B V32AS_BUILD_SCRIPT
which has the same effect, so a script that is running is not rewritten.
.TP
.BI \-\-build\-script\-dir " DIR"
Write the scripts in
.I DIR
(which must exist) instead.
.SS "Compatibility and diagnostics"
.TP
.B \-\-standard
Be the official assembler, exactly: no extension to the language, no extended
preprocessor, a leading zero means decimal, one input file only, and
.B \-v
prints the official stage messages.
See
.BR v32as\-language (7)
for the list.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-no\-warnings
Inhibit all warnings.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-step
Pause after each statement, waiting for Enter on the terminal (files only; has
no effect with
.BR \-\-standard ,
and is ignored with a warning when standard input is not a terminal).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Trace the assembly of every instruction (the encoded bit fields) to standard
error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
The words
.BR bios ,
.BR cart ,
.BR none ,
.BR binary ,
.BR step ,
.BR verbose
and
.BR help ,
as bare arguments, are accepted as the corresponding options unless a file of
that name exists.
.SH "THE DEBUG FILE"
Plain
.B \-g
writes one line per instruction:
.IR address,file,line ,
plus
.I ,label
for an instruction at which a label is defined (the first one).
The addresses are byte\-identical to the official assembler's
.BR "\-g program" .
.PP
When the source has comment lines of their own of the form
.B ;@src FILE:LINE
(or
.IR FILE:LINE:COL ;
this is what
.B v32lua \-g
and
.B v32cc \-g
write), the entries come from them instead: one entry per marker, at the
address of the first instruction after it, with the function as a fourth
column.
.B ;@src \-
means "no source": the first instruction after it gets the entry
.IR address,\-,0 .
FILE is split from LINE at the last colon, so a drive letter is fine.
A function starts at a
.B __function_NAME:
label (the column holds
.IR NAME )
and ends at a line
.B ;@fn __function_NAME ...
following its body, at a
.BR ";@src \-" ,
or at the end of the program; functions nest.
When the source says
.B ;@lang X
(a comment line of its own, before the first function; the first one counts)
with
.I X
other than
.BR lua ,
functions do not nest: the next top\-level
.B __function_NAME:
label ends the previous function, so a C compiler needs no
.B ;@src \-
or
.B ;@fn
after each body.
A
.B ;@fn
written above a label is never an end.
Instructions an applied marker does not start have no entry; no label is
written in marker mode.
.B \-\-map
entries win for the instructions the map names.
With
.B \-\-standard
the markers are plain comments.
.PP
When the source has
.B ;@lang X
and the entries come from the markers (not with
.BR \-\-map ),
the first line of the file is the comment
.BR "# lang X" .
Readers must skip every line that starts with
.BR # .
There is no such line without
.BR ;@lang .
.SH "THE VARS FILE"
With
.B \-g
and source lines of the form
.B ;@var NAME loc=... type=... [flags]
and
.B ;@scope {
and
.B ;@scope }
lines,
.IR OUTPUT .vars
is written: the locals as address ranges.
(An old file is removed when the source has no such lines.)
The addresses are as in the .debug file for the same
.IR MODE ;
END is the first address that is out of scope.
One CSV line each, in order of START, a block before its variables.
A line
.B ;@home FROM TO
(written by v32opt when it moves a variable) ends the record of every variable in scope whose home is FROM and starts one with the home TO
in the same block;
.B optimized\-out
as TO means the value is gone.
.PP
.nf
.RS
scope,START,END,DEPTH[,FUNCTION]
var,START,END,NAME,LOC,TYPE,FLAGS,DEPTH[,FUNCTION]
.RE
.fi
.PP
LOC is the
.B loc=
as written (for example
.BR BP\-3 ,
.BR R5 ,
.BR @0x20003000 ,
or
.B \-
for
.BR optimized\-out ),
except that a symbol is resolved: a label, a
.B label+N
or
.B label\-N
(N decimal or 0x...), or a name that was
.BR %define d
as a number or as one of those (for example
.BR "var_NAME" ),
is written as
.BI @0x ADDRESS
when it is known at the end of the assembly.
A label gives its address in the style of the START column of the
.B \-g
mode; a
.BR %define d
number is a plain value and is written as it is.
An unknown symbol stays as written (and warns under
.BR \-Wmarkers ).
TYPE is the
.B type=
(or
.BR \- ),
FLAGS the bare words after it joined by
.B +
(or
.BR \- ).
DEPTH is 0 for the function's own scope and 1, 2, ...  for blocks inside it.
A variable is in scope from the first instruction after its line until the
.B ;@scope }
of its block (or the end of the function), or until another
.B ;@var
of the same name in the same block.
Empty ranges are left out.
With
.B ;@lang X
the first line is
.BR "# lang X" ;
readers must skip lines that start with
.BR # .
.SH "THE RELOCATION FILE"
.B \-r
writes one line per word that holds an address resolved from a label (code or
data, including
.IR label+N )
or computed from the instruction's own address
.RB ( "MOV Rn, IP" ,
and the
.B IR
and
.B IV
patch addresses).
Plain numbers that merely look like addresses are never listed.
Lines are
.IR index,0xVALUE ,
sorted by ascending index without duplicates.
.I index
is the zero\-based word index into the image, not counting the 3\-word VBIN
header; the first instruction is word 0.
.I VALUE
is the value as assembled, at the base the image was assembled for, so a loader
that places the image at a new base adds the difference to those words.
If the source declares
.B datasym
(see below) every line has a third column,
.B C
for a code address (moves with the load address) and
.B D
for a reference to a datasym (moves with the base of the module's data).
.SH "THE SYMBOL FILE"
.B \-\-symbols
writes
.IR 0xADDRESS,name,kind ,
sorted by address.
.I kind
is
.B F
(code follows) or
.B D
(data follows, or nothing).
With
.B \-\-symbols\-all
kind
.B V
lines are added: the value of each
.B global_NAME
define in the address column, and
.I N,DATASYM+N,V
with the offset in the address column.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success (warnings do not change the status).
.TP
.B 1
An assembly or preprocessor error, or an input file that cannot be read.
(With
.BR \-\-standard ,
more than one input file.)
.TP
.B 2
A usage error (bad or conflicting options, or a missing argument), or an output
file that cannot be written.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B V32AS_BUILD_SCRIPT
When set, the build script options (\fB\-\-build\-scripts\fR) are ignored, as with
.BR \-\-no\-build\-scripts ;
the scripts set it for the programs they run.
.PP
The scripts themselves read
.BR V32AS ,
.BR V32OPT ,
.BR PACKROM ,
.B V32OPT_FLAGS
(the options for
.BR v32opt ,
default
.BR \-O3 ),
.B PNG2VIRCON
and
.B WAV2VIRCON
(the program to run in place of the one found in the
.BR PATH )
and
.B NO_OPT
(set: do not run
.BR v32opt ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR FILE .vbin
The default output for an input
.IR FILE .asm.
.TP
.IR FILE .v32ob
The default output with
.BR \-C .
.TP
.IR NAME .xml ", " NAME Opt.xml
The ROM definitions written by
.B \-\-rom\-xml
and
.BR \-\-build\-scripts .
.TP
.IR Make.sh ", " Make.bat
The build scripts written by
.BR \-\-build\-scripts .
.TP
.IR OUTPUT .debug ", " OUTPUT .vars ", " OUTPUT .sym
Written by
.BR \-g .
.TP
.I lib/v32as.inc, lib/control.inc
Macro libraries for
.BR %include ;
see
.BR v32as\-inc (3)
and
.BR v32as\-control (3).
They are found with
.BR "\-I lib" ;
.B "make install"
puts them in
.IR PREFIX /share/v32as/lib .
.SH EXAMPLES
Assemble a cartridge program (writes
.IR hello.vbin ):
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as hello.asm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Use the macro library, keep the debug and relocation files and the label table:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-I lib \-g \-r hello.rel \-\-symbols hello.sym hello.asm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Show what the preprocessor makes of the source:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-I lib \-E \-P hello.asm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Assemble for the BIOS, or for any address, or in the official language only:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-b bios.asm
v32as \-O 0x3000 \-o ram.bin code.asm
v32as \-\-standard game.asm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Command line definitions, and a rule for make:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-DDEBUG \-DLEVEL=3 \-MD \-MP game.asm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Separate assembly and linking:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-C a.asm
v32as \-C b.asm
v32asld \-o prog.vbin a.v32ob b.v32ob
.RE
.fi
.PP
A V32X executable for v32nix, 16 zeroed data words behind the program:
.PP
.nf
.RS
v32as \-\-origin 0x100000 \-\-v32x \-\-v32x\-data 16 \-o prog.v32x prog.asm
.RE
.fi
.PP
Assemble from standard input:
.PP
.nf
.RS
echo ' hlt' | v32as \- > out.vbin
.RE
.fi
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostics have the form
.IR file:line[:column]:\ error:\ message
or
.IR "warning: message" .
Typical warnings: a number with a leading zero is octal here but decimal in the
official assembler; a port number that is not a known I/O port or does not fit
in 14 bits; code that patches itself
.RB ( "MOV IR" ", " "MOV IV" )
outside RAM; a
.B %define
that redefines a
.B \-D
name; a macro that has the same name as an instruction.
.PP
After a preprocessor error the program is not assembled, and no undefined
label complaints are added.
.SH NOTES
A label starts with an underscore.
Mnemonics, registers and directive names are case\-insensitive; macro names are
not.
.PP
.B datasym NAME
(enhanced syntax) declares a name whose value is 0, so
.I NAME+17
is a word holding 17 that is marked
.B D
in the relocation file.
.B v32cc \-\-pic\-data
writes every global's address that way.
.SH LIMITS
Include depth is limited to 20 levels, macro and
.B %rep
nesting to 64 levels, a
.B %rep
to 1,000,000 iterations, and a run to about 4 million replayed macro or
.B %rep
body lines.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR v32asld (1),
.BR v32as\-language (7),
.BR v32as\-inc (3),
.BR v32as\-control (3),
.BR v32cc (1)
.PP
README.md, docs/PREPROCESSOR.md and docs/OBJECT.md in the source distribution.
